WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:06.560
Okay, so now what is exactly a full antijoin?

00:06.560 --> 00:08.200
Well, this time we don't have sides.

00:08.200 --> 00:13.040
We want to return only the rows that don't match in either tables.

00:13.040 --> 00:17.840
So what this means if you are looking to the left circle, we want only the unmatching rows.

00:17.840 --> 00:19.440
So we don't want the whole circle.

00:19.440 --> 00:24.840
We want only the data that exists in A but it don't exist in B on the right table.

00:24.880 --> 00:29.440
Sounds like the left antijoin but since we are saying full, then you have to do the same thing on the

00:29.440 --> 00:30.240
right side as well.

00:30.240 --> 00:33.800
So on the right table we want only the unmatching rows.

00:33.800 --> 00:38.840
So we want to see in the result the data that is in B but don't have a match from A.

00:38.880 --> 00:40.160
So it's exactly the opposite.

00:40.160 --> 00:44.600
And if you look to this then that means we want to see only the unmatching data.

00:44.600 --> 00:48.320
And this is exactly the opposite effect of the inner join.

00:48.360 --> 00:53.360
If the inner join we were interested only on the matching data, only when there is like overlapping.

00:53.360 --> 00:56.040
But now with the full antijoin it is exactly the opposite.

00:56.040 --> 01:00.850
We don't want to see the matching data, we want to see everything else, the unmatching data.

01:00.850 --> 01:02.730
So how we can write this query again?

01:02.770 --> 01:05.490
Here we don't have a special type called full Antijoin.

01:05.490 --> 01:08.050
We will use the help of the classical full join.

01:08.050 --> 01:08.930
So the basic one.

01:08.930 --> 01:12.130
So you start from a full join B and then the same key.

01:12.170 --> 01:14.890
But now what is interesting is about the where condition.

01:14.890 --> 01:16.930
Now we have like two conditions right.

01:16.970 --> 01:22.090
So now in order to get all data from a that has no match in B, you have to make a filter where you

01:22.090 --> 01:24.850
say the key from the B table must be null.

01:24.890 --> 01:29.530
And now, since we want the exact same thing from the right table, we want all the data in B that has

01:29.530 --> 01:30.530
no match in A.

01:30.650 --> 01:32.330
You have to say as well.

01:32.530 --> 01:35.010
The key from the A table must be null.

01:35.050 --> 01:36.850
So now we have here like two conditions.

01:36.850 --> 01:41.650
And in SQL if you have like two conditions in the Where clause, you have here two options either use

01:41.690 --> 01:44.770
and operator or the or operator.

01:44.770 --> 01:48.090
So now the one that we're going to use here is the or operator.

01:48.090 --> 01:51.970
So either the key from right is empty or the key from left is empty.

01:52.010 --> 01:55.970
If you do it like this you will get the effect of the full antijoin.

01:55.970 --> 02:01.730
And of course, since here both sides are equal, then the order of the tables as well here is not that

02:01.730 --> 02:02.290
important.

02:02.290 --> 02:06.970
So you can say from a full join B or from B full join A, it doesn't matter.

02:06.970 --> 02:09.970
So now let's go back to SQL in order to create this effect.

02:10.010 --> 02:10.170
Okay.

02:10.250 --> 02:11.450
So now we have the following task.

02:11.450 --> 02:16.450
And it says find customers without orders and orders without customers.

02:16.450 --> 02:21.970
So if you are looking to this this means we want to see only the Unmatching data from customers and

02:21.970 --> 02:23.130
as well from orders.

02:23.130 --> 02:25.290
There is no main table and secondary table.

02:25.330 --> 02:27.050
Both of them are equally important.

02:27.050 --> 02:32.010
So now since we are talking about the Unmatching data and the Antijoin, we have to do it in two steps.

02:32.010 --> 02:35.850
The first step, we're going to do the classical join and then we focus on the Where clause.

02:35.850 --> 02:38.970
So let me remove the Where clause to make it as a comment.

02:39.010 --> 02:43.010
Now since we want the data from left and right, we're going to go and use the full join.

02:43.010 --> 02:44.370
So let's go and execute it.

02:44.370 --> 02:46.810
Now you can see we are getting the effect of the full join.

02:46.810 --> 02:50.170
We are getting all the orders and as well all the customers.

02:50.170 --> 02:56.370
But now we are interested only on the strange cases where there are like orders without customers like

02:56.370 --> 02:59.260
this one here and as well customers without orders.

02:59.260 --> 03:04.180
So that means the first three rows are not really interesting for us because it is boring.

03:04.180 --> 03:08.820
We have here matching data and this is totally fine, but we are not focusing on that now.

03:08.820 --> 03:14.700
We are focusing only if there is like missing data from left or from right, as you notice I'm saying.

03:14.740 --> 03:17.140
Or and this is very important because we're going to use the Or.

03:17.180 --> 03:17.700
Operator.

03:17.700 --> 03:20.340
So now let's focus on getting this scenario over here.

03:20.340 --> 03:22.940
We want to get an order without a customer.

03:22.940 --> 03:26.100
So that means the customer ID must be null.

03:26.100 --> 03:27.260
And we have it already here.

03:27.260 --> 03:30.860
So we are saying where the ID of the customer is null.

03:30.860 --> 03:35.420
So if I go and execute it I will get only one records, only this one over here.

03:35.420 --> 03:38.500
But as well I want to get the opposite scenario.

03:38.500 --> 03:41.900
So in this scenario the customer ID must be null.

03:41.900 --> 03:47.900
So we're going to say or the customer ID in the orders is null.

03:48.060 --> 03:50.180
Or we can do it like side by side like this.

03:50.180 --> 03:53.700
Either the right side is null or the left side is null.

03:53.700 --> 03:57.660
So if you go and execute it, you will get the effect of the full Antijoin.

03:57.660 --> 04:02.500
And with that, we are finding the customers without orders and orders without customers.

04:02.540 --> 04:05.220
I think this is really fun and as well, really easy.

04:05.220 --> 04:07.460
So this is how we do the full antijoin.

04:07.460 --> 04:07.780
All right.

04:07.780 --> 04:11.420
So now if you are looking to the use cases, we use the full Antijoin again.

04:11.460 --> 04:12.020
Exactly.

04:12.020 --> 04:15.100
For the last use case in order to check the existence.

04:15.100 --> 04:20.420
So if you combine the full with the where, you can check the existence or the not existence of your

04:20.420 --> 04:21.940
data in another table.

04:21.980 --> 04:24.060
So this is exactly the scenario for that.

04:28.020 --> 04:32.380
Okay my friends, now we have a bonus section where I'm going to challenge you to solve the following

04:32.420 --> 04:34.860
task without using an inner join.

04:34.860 --> 04:41.300
So it says get all customers along with their orders, but only for customers who have placed an order

04:41.300 --> 04:43.740
but without using an inner join.

04:43.740 --> 04:46.580
So pause the video now and go and solve this task.

04:49.580 --> 04:51.740
Okay, so now let's see how we can solve this.

04:51.780 --> 04:57.020
We want the customers, the orders, blah blah blah, but we want only the customers who have placed

04:57.020 --> 04:57.670
an order.

04:57.670 --> 05:01.950
Previously, we have used the inner join in order to solve this task, but this time we are not allowed

05:01.950 --> 05:02.510
