05:02.510 --> 05:03.750
So let's go and solve it.

05:03.750 --> 05:04.790
This is how I'm going to do it.

05:04.790 --> 05:08.950
Select star from table customers and give it the alias.

05:08.950 --> 05:10.750
So now I'm getting all the customers.

05:10.750 --> 05:14.870
But I am interested only the customers who have placed an order.

05:14.870 --> 05:19.150
So as we know before, there is like two customers that didn't order anything and we don't want to see

05:19.150 --> 05:20.670
them in the final results.

05:20.710 --> 05:22.030
Now how we will get that?

05:22.070 --> 05:27.550
Well, we can use the help of the table orders in order to check the existence of our customers there.

05:27.590 --> 05:31.830
And of course, I'm not allowed to use the inner join, so I'm going to go and use a left join with

05:31.830 --> 05:34.830
the table orders and then combine them as usual.

05:34.830 --> 05:38.550
Nothing new with the customer ID, so now let's go and execute it.

05:38.550 --> 05:40.550
As you can see, we are doing it step by step.

05:40.550 --> 05:42.830
You don't have to rush everything in one go.

05:42.830 --> 05:46.710
So you start simple, check the results and decide on the next step.

05:46.710 --> 05:51.710
So now by looking to these results I want to get those three customers because they have ordered something

05:51.710 --> 05:56.350
and we are seeing data about their orders and I don't want to get in the result the last two.

05:56.390 --> 06:02.990
So again, we still can use the customer ID from the right table in order to decide which data are going

06:02.990 --> 06:05.350
to stay in the result and which data should be filtered.

06:05.350 --> 06:09.430
We're going to go and use the Where clause and then the key from the orders.

06:09.430 --> 06:12.430
And this time we're going to say is not null.

06:12.470 --> 06:18.950
I know we didn't learn yet about the nut and the logical operators, but using the not null it means

06:18.950 --> 06:20.950
they should be data inside the column.

06:20.950 --> 06:22.310
It must not be null.

06:22.310 --> 06:27.510
If you do it like this and executes, you will get the exact effect as the inner join.

06:27.510 --> 06:32.110
So as you can see, as you are joining the tables using the left join, you can control what you want

06:32.110 --> 06:34.990
to see using the Where clause using the filter.

06:35.030 --> 06:38.510
And this is how you can solve this task without using an inner join.

06:38.550 --> 06:43.230
Okay, so with that, we have covered all the three scenarios in order to find the unmatched data.

06:43.270 --> 06:45.550
